}

{
  "analysis": "The frotz process appears to have exited (likely a death mid-fight combined with a glitch sent commands to the bash shell), so we're now at a bash prompt in /app/frotz. Importantly, my earlier SAVE to 'cellar.sav' should still exist, giving me a safe restore point right before the Troll Room. I'll relaunch frotz, restore that save, and retry the troll fight. I must be careful to keep commands flowing only into frotz.",
  "plan": "1) Relaunch frotz and dismiss the title screen with a newline. 2) Verify I'm at the prompt. Then (next batch) RESTORE from cellar.sav and fight the troll, retrying via restore on any death. First just relaunch and confirm the prompt is ready.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "./frotz zork1.z5\n", "duration": 1.2},
    {"keystrokes": "\n", "duration": 0.5},
    {"keystrokes": "restore\n", "duration": 0.5},
    {"keystrokes": "cellar.sav\n", "duration": 0.5},
    {"keystrokes": "look\n", "duration": 0.3}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The frotz process had exited (the previous batch's keystrokes landed in bash due to a JSON parsing hiccup). I relaunched frotz and tried to RESTORE from cellar.sav, but it reported 'Failed.' — so either the file isn't there or it's invalid. I need to inspect the filesystem to understand the save situation. Note: the troll fight caused a death earlier and the troll must be killed for the score, so I need a reliable save/restore workflow. Let me quit to bash and check what save files exist.",
  "plan": "Quit frotz, then list files in /app/frotz to see if cellar.sav exists and is valid. This will tell me whether my save persists. Based on that I'll decide how to proceed with retrying the troll fight safely.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "quit\n", "duration": 0.4},
    {"keystrokes": "y\n", "duration": 0.5},
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la\n", "duration": 0.3},
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la *.sav *.qzl 2>/dev/null\n", "duration": 0.3}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Two problems: (1) My earlier SAVE 'cellar.sav' did not persist (the ls of *.sav found nothing), so that restore point is gone. (2) frotz now refuses to run as root ('I won't run as root!'), which is why the relaunch dropped back to bash. The terminal is at a bash prompt in /app/frotz as root. I need to find a way to run frotz as a non-root user, then replay the early game and use SAVE/RESTORE to safely retry the troll fight. First I must diagnose the environment.",
  "plan": "Diagnose: confirm I'm root, inspect the frotz binary, and find a usable non-root user. Then I'll run frotz as that user (likely via sudo -u or su). I'll check for sudo, and look at /etc/passwd for a normal user account to drop privileges into.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "id\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la frotz\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "file frotz\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "which sudo su\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "grep -E ':/bin/(ba)?sh' /etc/passwd\n", "duration": 0.2}
